---
/**
 * The Insights index — item 2 in the phase 4 order.
 *
 * ── THE MASONRY IS PARTITIONED HERE, NOT LAID OUT BY CSS ─────────────────────
 *
 * Native CSS masonry does not ship, and the two things that do work today each cost
 * something. The full reasoning and the measurements are in src/lib/masonry.ts.
 *
 * ON BALANCE THE TWO ARE CLOSE, and an earlier version of this comment claimed
 * otherwise. Measured on this page, at 1440, with the real 45 documents:
 *
 *   explicit columns   246px ragged (3.7%)   top row reads items 0, 1, 2
 *   column-count: 3    340px ragged (5.1%)   top row reads items 0, 16, 30
 *
 * A "three times better" figure appeared here first and was an artefact of testing
 * both against SYNTHETIC divs built from estimated heights. The real margin is about
 * 1.4x, and even that owes something to luck: greedy fed the TRUE rendered heights
 * would produce 420px. The residual is the granularity of ~400px indivisible cards,
 * not estimator error, and no better estimator improves it.
 *
 * SO THE REASON FOR REAL COLUMNS IS READING ORDER, NOT BALANCE — 0, 1, 2 against
 * 0, 16, 30 — plus the fact that revealing more cards appends to column ends instead
 * of rebalancing the whole grid, which is what "show more" needs.
 *
 * ── WHICH MAKES THE BREAKPOINT LADDER THE INTERESTING PART ───────────────────
 *
 * One DOM can serve exactly ONE partition. The partition below is computed for three
 * columns, so three columns is the only rung it can express — and every narrower rung
 * has to get the cards back out of it. `display: contents` does that: it erases the
 * column boxes while keeping their children, so the cards become grid items of
 * `.masonry` itself. Same mechanism the article page's rail uses.
 *
 * ── SOURCE ORDER IS PARTITION ORDER, WHICH IS WHY `order` IS PASSED ─────────
 *
 * An earlier draft of this comment said the erased wrappers let the cards "fall into
 * source order, which is date order". FALSE — the partition physically reorders the
 * DOM. Measured at 400px, the cards rendered in this date-index sequence:
 *
 *     0, 4, 6, 9, 12 … 42,   1, 3, 7, 10 … 43,   2, 5, 8 … 44
 *
 * So the single-column rung read down column one and then jumped back to the
 * second-newest item — the exact top-to-bottom-then-across flaw that ruled
 * `column-count` out in the first place, reappearing at the narrow rungs.
 *
 * FIXED VISUALLY by handing each card its date index as CSS `order`. One declaration
 * in each card, no breakpoint: at `lg` the values inside a column wrapper are already
 * ascending so sorting by them changes nothing, and below `lg` the same values put the
 * erased cards back in date order.
 *
 * ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 * │  WHAT `order` DOES NOT FIX, AND IT IS NOT FIXABLE HERE.                  │
 * │                                                                          │
 * │  It reorders the BOX TREE only. Focus order, screen-reader traversal and  │
 * │  microformats parsing all follow the DOM, which stays in partition order  │
 * │  at every width. So this trades a consistent-but-wrong order for a        │
 * │  correct visual order and a visual-versus-FOCUS MISMATCH — the trade      │
 * │  DESIGN.md's Responsive section warns about for bands containing          │
 * │  controls, and a card contains a link. Taken knowingly (Andy, 2026-08-28).│
 * │                                                                          │
 * │  There is no third option while the columns are real elements. For        │
 * │  wrapper one to hold DOM items 0-14, the top row must read 0, 15, 30 —    │
 * │  contiguous DOM grouping and row-major visual order are the SAME          │
 * │  constraint inverted. Row-major requires either DOM != date order, or     │
 * │  `order`. It is a property of the structure, not a gap in the build.      │
 * └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
 *
 * The consequence, and it is a real one: the two-column rung is a ROW GRID, not a
 * second masonry, because source order into two tracks is what `display: contents`
 * leaves behind. A card that is shorter than its row-mate leaves empty space below
 * itself rather than stretching — which is what `align-items: start` is for, and it is
 * load-bearing rather than tidy. Without it an unboxed note card beside a tall article
 * card would reveal a box the article's height on hover.
 *
 * ── AND WHY NO SCRIPT RESCUES THE MIDDLE RUNG ────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Re-partitioning in the browser on a breakpoint crossing sounds like the fix and is
 * not: someone LOADING the page at 800px would get the three-column partition painted
 * into a two-track grid before any script ran. The CSS has to be correct at every
 * width on its own, so the middle rung is a row grid and that is the design.
 *
 * Filtering is the different case, and the one where a client re-partition IS wanted —
 * a build-time partition balances against the whole set, and 400 random filter
 * selections left it 800-1300px ragged at the median against ~300 when re-partitioned
 * live. That lands with the chips, not here.
 */
import {loadQuery} from '../../sanity/load-query'
import {INSIGHTS_INDEX_QUERY} from '../../sanity/queries/insights'
import {SINGLETON_HEADER_QUERY} from '../../sanity/queries/singletons'
import {partitionColumns, estimateCardHeight} from '../../lib/masonry'
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Band from '../../components/Band.astro'
import Grid from '../../components/Grid.astro'
import Masthead from '../../components/Masthead.astro'
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro'
import Prose from '../../components/prose/Prose.astro'
import ArticleCard from '../../components/cards/ArticleCard.astro'
import NoteCard from '../../components/cards/NoteCard.astro'

/** The rung the partition is computed for. See the ladder in the style block. */
const MASONRY_COLUMNS = 3

/* Both fetches are independent, so they go together rather than in series. */
const [page, insights] = await Promise.all([
  loadQuery(SINGLETON_HEADER_QUERY, {slug: 'insights'}),
  loadQuery(INSIGHTS_INDEX_QUERY),
])

/* Loud rather than quiet, matching `[slug].astro`. The singleton is content, so it can
   be unpublished or re-slugged in the Studio — and a page that silently lost its own
   title and lead would look like a styling bug rather than a missing document. */
if (!page) throw new Error('[insights/index] no singleton with slug "insights"')

/**
 * One flat shape per card, resolved BEFORE the partition rather than inside the
 * template.
 *
 * Two reasons it happens here. The estimator wants the strings the card will actually
 * render — a Clipping shows its source domain where a Note shows its description — so
 * resolving them once means the height model and the markup cannot disagree about what
 * is on the card. And the template then does no data work at all beyond choosing a
 * component.
 *
 * The query filters on `defined(slug.current)`, but TypeGen types a projected slug as
 * `string | null` because it cannot see the filter — so this narrows rather than
 * asserting, exactly as `[slug].astro` does for `getStaticPaths`.
 */
const ordered = insights.flatMap((item) => {
  if (!item.slug) return []

  const isNote = item._type === 'note'
  const sourceDomain = isNote ? (item.clipRef?.publisher ?? null) : null

  return [
    {
      id: item._id,
      isNote,
      href: `/insights/${item.slug}/`,
      title: item.title ?? 'Untitled',
      genre: item.genre,
      date: item.pubDate,
      description: item.shortDescription,
      image: item.heroImage,
      sourceDomain,
      /* What the estimator measures: the body a Clipping renders is its domain, not
         its description, and a note never carries media. */
      body: sourceDomain ?? item.shortDescription,
      hasMedia: Boolean(item.heroImage?.asset),
    },
  ]
})

/**
 * The date index, assigned AFTER the slug filter so it is dense — 0..n-1 with no
 * holes. It is what each card hands to CSS `order`, so a hole would leave a gap in
 * the sort rather than an error, which is the kind of thing nothing would report.
 *
 * Taken from this array and not from the query result's index for the same reason.
 */
const cards = ordered.map((card, index) => ({...card, order: index}))

/**
 * Greedy, shortest-column-first, walking newest to oldest.
 *
 * Prefix-balanced by construction — the columns are approximately level after EVERY
 * placement, not just the last — which is what will make "show more" free when it
 * arrives: revealing the tail of each column keeps the balance without a second
 * partition. Measured on the real corpus: 105-157px ragged at most prefix lengths
 * against a 6000px column.
 *
 * ONE ARITHMETIC TRAP FOR THAT LATER WORK. A reveal count that is not a multiple of
 * MASONRY_COLUMNS lands the remainder on one column and undoes the balance — the first
 * 20 measured 413px ragged where the first 18 and first 21 measured 105 and 134. The
 * increment wants to be a multiple of the column count.
 */
const columns = partitionColumns(cards, MASONRY_COLUMNS, estimateCardHeight)
---

<BaseLayout title={page.title ?? 'Insights'} description={page.lede ?? undefined}>
  <Masthead />

  <main>
    <Band as="section" class="index-band">
      <Grid>
        {
          /*
          ── STILL INLINE, NOT YET A <PageHeader> ─────────────────────────────

          DESIGN.md calls the page header "one component with an optional lead", and
          this is the first thing to render the optional half. It stays inline anyway,
          for the reason the project promotes on: a SECOND CONSUMER is the trigger, and
          there is not one yet. The article page's title block looks like it, but it is
          an eyebrow above an h1 with no lead — a different shape, and extracting a
          component around both today would be guessing at what they share.

          Reviews, Presentations and Home all want exactly this pair. Whichever lands
          first is the second consumer, and that is when it moves.

          A <div> around the lead rather than a bare <Prose>: the component is
          deliberately TRANSPARENT — it emits a flat sequence of blocks and no
          container — so `heroCopy`'s paragraphs would otherwise become children of
          <header> with nothing to scope the lead role to.
        */
        }
        <header class="page-header">
          <h1>{page.title ?? 'Insights'}</h1>
          {
            page.heroCopy && (
              <div class="page-lede">
                <Prose value={page.heroCopy} />
              </div>
            )
          }
        </header>

        {
          /*
          Filters land between the header and the masonry, at `1 / span 7` and
          `9 / span 4` — the same asymmetric split as Home, measured off the board.
          <Grid>'s own `rhythm-heading-major` row gap is the 64 the board draws
          between all three blocks, so nothing here sets vertical spacing.
        */
        }

        <div class="masonry">
          {
            columns.map((column) => (
              <div class="masonry-column">
                {column.map((card) =>
                  card.isNote ? (
                    <NoteCard
                      as="h2"
                      order={card.order}
                      href={card.href}
                      title={card.title}
                      genre={card.genre}
                      date={card.date}
                      description={card.description}
                      sourceDomain={card.sourceDomain}
                    />
                  ) : (
                    <ArticleCard
                      as="h2"
                      order={card.order}
                      href={card.href}
                      title={card.title}
                      genre={card.genre}
                      date={card.date}
                      description={card.description}
                      image={card.image}
                    />
                  ),
                )}
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </Grid>
    </Band>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* The header sits on the prose measure; the masonry spans the full content width.
     They therefore cannot share a grid row, so <Grid>'s row gap separates them — which
     is `rhythm-heading-major`, and correct here for the reason it is always correct:
     what lands below starts with a heading. */
  .page-header {
    grid-column: var(--col-main);
  }

  /*
   * ── DESIGN.md's `lead` ROLE, SCOPED HERE ON PURPOSE ───────────────────────
   *
   * base.css states its own scope: h1-h4, body prose, links, nav, blockquote, figure,
   * icons and the rhythm mechanism, and "every other typographic role in DESIGN.md
   * (display, lead, small, label, …) is component-scoped and belongs to the component
   * that owns it." The page header owns the lead, so it lives here — and moves with it
   * when the header becomes a real component.
   *
   * Noto Serif at the `font-size-3` step, weight 400, in `text-lead`. Family and weight
   * are inherited from body rather than restated: `--font-prose` at 400 is already what
   * a <p> gets, so declaring them again would be two more places to drift.
   *
   * LEADING 1.6 IS THE POINT OF THE ROLE, not the size. DESIGN.md indexes leading by
   * MEASURE, not by size step — 1.5 at ~29-33 characters, 1.6 at ~54, 1.8 at ~67 — and
   * a lead sits at the middle register. `body` at 1.8 here would be leading tuned for a
   * line a third longer than this one actually runs.
   */
  .page-lede {
    color: var(--color-text-lead);
    font-size: var(--font-size-3);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  /* What follows an h1, which base.css's ramp does not cover — it handles h2-h4 and
     leaves the h1 out. DESIGN.md settled this on the article page: `heading-close`,
     because the lead is bound to the title the way a paragraph is bound to a heading. */
  .page-header h1 + .page-lede {
    margin-top: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
  }

  /*
   * ── A LEAD IS ONE PARAGRAPH, AND NOTHING HERE SPACES A SECOND ──────────────
   *
   * A draft of this file carried `.page-lede :global(p + p)` giving a second paragraph
   * `rhythm-paragraph`, on the grounds that `heroCopy` is an array and About currently
   * holds two blocks. REMOVED DELIBERATELY (Andy, 2026-08-28): the second About
   * paragraph is body copy that reads as body copy on the live site only because it was
   * styled to, and it moves into `bodyText` when that page is built. A lead is one
   * paragraph.
   *
   * The rule is not merely unnecessary — it is the mechanism by which the drift
   * happened. Styling that makes a second paragraph look intentional is exactly what
   * stops anyone noticing the content model has been worked around. Without it a second
   * block butts against the first with no space, which is visibly wrong and is the
   * signal we want.
   *
   * The right home for the invariant is a `validation: Rule.max(1)` on `heroCopy` in the
   * Studio, so the editor cannot create the state at all. That is schema work and
   * belongs with the About page, not here.
   */

  /*
   * ── THE LADDER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * DRAFT — this is the part CLAUDE.md puts in Andy's column, written to be argued
   * with rather than accepted. Three rungs, mobile-first, and the breakpoint numbers
   * stay in tokens.css: these read the `--breakpoint-*` flags rather than restating
   * `48rem` and `64rem`.
   *
   * Kept as style container queries rather than a `--masonry-columns` token because
   * the rung changes TWO things that have to move together — the track count and
   * whether the column boxes exist at all — and only the first is a value. Splitting
   * it would put half the ladder in tokens.css and half here.
   */
  .masonry {
    grid-column: var(--col-full);

    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;

    /* Horizontal is grid, vertical is rhythm. The board draws 24 between columns and
       32 between cards, which is exactly this pair. */
    column-gap: var(--grid-gutter);
    row-gap: var(--rhythm-list);

    /*
     * NOT DECORATIVE, AND IT APPLIES AT EVERY RUNG.
     *
     * At `lg` it stops the three column boxes stretching to the tallest. Below `lg`
     * the column boxes are gone and the CARDS are the grid items, where stretching
     * would equalise every row — and an unboxed note card stretched to a tall
     * article card's height would reveal that whole box on hover.
     *
     * Set on the container rather than per card deliberately: `display: contents`
     * changes the box tree and not the selector tree, so `.masonry > *` still matches
     * the erased wrapper. `align-items` reaches whatever the real grid items are.
     */
    align-items: start;
  }

  /*
   * Erased below `lg`, so the cards become grid items of `.masonry` in SOURCE ORDER —
   * which is date order, and is why the narrow rungs need no second partition. The
   * boxes come back at `lg`, where the build-time partition applies.
   */
  .masonry-column {
    display: contents;
  }

  @container style(--breakpoint-md: true) {
    .masonry {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @container style(--breakpoint-lg: true) {
    .masonry {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }

    /* A flex column rather than block flow, so the 32 between cards is one `gap`
       instead of a sibling-margin rule. Portable Text's flat-sequence constraint —
       the reason the article body uses margins — does not apply to a list of
       components we are emitting ourselves. */
    .masonry-column {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: var(--rhythm-list);
    }
  }
</style>
